1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,912
What is gradient descent really doing if we stop it early?

2
00:00:09,912 --> 00:00:13,176
To see it, we rotate everything into the SVD basis.

3
00:00:13,176 --> 00:00:20,700
Now each direction runs its own little recurrence on a single number, with the same shape we saw in episode one.

4
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:23,223
Start at zero and solve that recurrence.

5
00:00:23,223 --> 00:00:29,279
After any number of steps, each direction has gone some fraction of the way to its final answer,

6
00:00:29,279 --> 00:00:32,244
and that fraction is the early-stopping filter.

7
00:00:32,244 --> 00:00:37,733
Once the filter reaches one, the direction is fully converged.

8
00:00:37,733 --> 00:00:40,640
Let's plot that filter against the singular value.

9
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:45,988
After a few steps only the strong directions are fit, and the weak ones have barely started.

10
00:00:45,988 --> 00:00:53,000
Keep going and the curve climbs in from the right, so strong directions finish first while weak ones take ages.

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,086
Now put the ridge filter next to one of these curves.

12
00:00:56,086 --> 00:01:01,386
Both hold back the weak directions, so stopping early regularizes without any penalty term.

13
00:01:01,386 --> 00:01:03,424
They aren't the same curve, though.

14
00:01:03,424 --> 00:01:09,233
What they share is the order, with weak directions always coming in last.

15
00:01:09,233 --> 00:01:10,866
Is that order guaranteed?

16
00:01:10,866 --> 00:01:12,826
Only when eta is small enough.

17
00:01:12,826 --> 00:01:17,790
For a direction with sigma one point five, an eta of zero point one is safe,

18
00:01:17,790 --> 00:01:20,664
and the filter rises to one and stays there.

19
00:01:20,664 --> 00:01:29,766
Push eta to zero point three and it's still stable, but now the filter overshoots past one before it settles back.

20
00:01:29,766 --> 00:01:31,255
So when should we stop?

21
00:01:31,255 --> 00:01:38,182
Here are four directions, from a strong one with singular value three down to a weak one at zero point one,

22
00:01:38,182 --> 00:01:39,606
and the data is noisy.

23
00:01:39,606 --> 00:01:46,500
The training loss only ever goes down, and given enough steps it fits every direction, noise and all.

24
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:53,068
But the distance from the true weights falls and then climbs again, as the weak, noisy directions get fit.

25
00:01:53,068 --> 00:01:55,547
The sweet spot is near thirty-six steps.

26
00:01:55,547 --> 00:02:00,319
If we run all the way to convergence the error is about fourteen times worse,

27
00:02:00,319 --> 00:02:04,266
so stopping early buys a little bias for a lot less noise.

28
00:02:04,266 --> 00:02:08,656
In practice you can't see the true weights, so you save checkpoints as you train.

29
00:02:08,656 --> 00:02:13,900
Then you keep whichever checkpoint scores best on validation data.

30
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:20,955
That leaves us three hyperparameters to choose, which are the ridge lambda, the learning rate and the stopping time.

31
00:02:20,955 --> 00:02:25,335
Each one can range over many powers of ten, so we search on a log scale.

32
00:02:25,335 --> 00:02:33,700
Evenly spaced values bunch up inside a single decade, while one value per power of ten covers the whole range.

33
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:38,303
Each candidate is trained on the training set and then scored on validation data.

34
00:02:38,303 --> 00:02:44,800
The test set stays locked away until the very end, when the whole procedure is fixed.

35
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:46,444
A full grid blows up fast.

36
00:02:46,444 --> 00:02:53,337
With five values for each hyperparameter, that's five runs, then twenty-five, then a hundred and twenty-five,

37
00:02:53,337 --> 00:02:56,372
and with six of them it's over fifteen thousand.

38
00:02:56,372 --> 00:03:05,233
Random search draws a fresh value of every hyperparameter each run, so the same budget usually covers more ground.

39
00:03:05,233 --> 00:03:10,523
And if there's no budget to search at all, you can borrow settings from a closely related paper,

40
00:03:10,523 --> 00:03:12,728
as a starting guess rather than a proof.

41
00:03:12,728 --> 00:03:15,042
Could a learner tune these for us instead?

42
00:03:15,042 --> 00:03:22,166
Meta-learning can, but that's an outer loop with a cost of its own.

